{%- comment -%}
  research-methodology-callout
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Render at the end of every product review and buyer's guide. Lands the
  E-E-A-T "how we research products" methodology on every editorial page —
  not only on the dedicated /pages/editorial-methodology page.

  Usage in a blog article template (e.g. sections/main-article.liquid),
  just below {{ article.content }}:

      {% render 'research-methodology-callout',
                reviewer: article.metafields.editorial.reviewer,
                last_reviewed: article.metafields.editorial.last_reviewed %}

  Or with literal arguments per-article:

      {% render 'research-methodology-callout',
                reviewer: 'Jane Doe, CIH',
                last_reviewed: '2026-06-14' %}

  If no reviewer/last_reviewed is supplied, the snippet falls back to
  'WC Safety Editorial Team' and the article's published_at date. To wire
  real names per article, create two article metafields under namespace
  'editorial': 'reviewer' (single line text), 'last_reviewed' (date).
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign callout_reviewer = reviewer | default: 'WC Safety Editorial Team' -%}
{%- assign callout_date = last_reviewed | default: article.published_at -%}

<aside class="wc-research-callout" aria-label="How this review was researched">
  <h2 class="wc-research-callout__title">How this review was researched</h2>

  <p>
    This article was built against primary sources: the
    <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/npptl/topics/respirators/cel/default.html"
       rel="noopener">NIOSH Certified Equipment List</a>
    for any respirator and cartridge TC-approval cross-reference,
    <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.134"
       rel="noopener">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.134</a>
    for workplace respiratory-protection program requirements, and the relevant
    ANSI/ASSE standards, UL listings, and manufacturer technical data sheets.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>What we did:</strong>
    hands-on inspection of the unit we stock, on-unit verification of the
    certification markings against the source database, and specification
    cross-reference against the governing standard. Photographs on this page
    are of the actual product, taken by us.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>What we did not do:</strong>
    destructive testing, laboratory filtration-efficiency testing, or
    quantitative fit-factor measurement. Where a conclusion depends on data
    only a lab or field test can produce, we cite NIOSH or the manufacturer
    rather than imply we measured it ourselves.
  </p>

  <p>
    This article is not a substitute for a workplace respiratory-protection
    program or a Certified Industrial Hygienist's assessment. For
    program-level decisions, engage a qualified safety professional. For
    suspected acute exposure or respiratory symptoms, contact a physician,
    urgent care, or U.S. Poison Control at <strong>1-800-222-1222</strong>.
  </p>

  <p class="wc-research-callout__meta">
    <strong>Reviewed by:</strong> {{ callout_reviewer }} ·
    <strong>Last reviewed:</strong> {{ callout_date | date: '%B %-d, %Y' }} ·
    <a href="/pages/editorial-methodology">Full editorial methodology</a> ·
    <a href="/pages/affiliate-disclosure">Affiliate disclosure</a>
  </p>
</aside>

{%- comment -%}
  Optional minimal CSS — drop into theme.css / base.css, scoped to the
  callout class so it inherits but is distinguishable from body text:

  .wc-research-callout {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border, #ddd);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
    background: var(--color-background-subtle, #f7f7f7);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .wc-research-callout__title { margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .wc-research-callout__meta { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.875rem; }
{%- endcomment -%}
